## Initial Q&A
### Did the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania officially reject mail-in ballots for the 2020 general election?
Answer: Based on the search result, the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania's official guidance for the 2020 general election was that counties were "not allowed to reject a ballot solely because an election official believes a signature doesn't match the signature in the voter’s file." This guidance was issued to address concerns and prevent the mass rejection of ballots over technicalities. The article does not support the claim that the state officially rejected 372,000 mail-in ballots for the general election.

Source URL: https://apnews.com/article/pennsylvania-election-2020-pittsburgh-elections-presidential-elections-fc464c287c18823ff57fedc13facf7e5

### What was the official number of mail-in ballots rejected by Pennsylvania authorities for the 2020 general election?
Answer: According to the official report from ProPublica and The Philadelphia Inquirer, Pennsylvania authorities rejected 372,000 **requests for mail-in ballots** (i.e., ballot applications) for the 2020 general election, primarily because they were duplicates.

Source URL: https://www.propublica.org/article/pennsylvanias-rejection-of-372-000-ballot-applications-bewilders-voters-and-strains-election-staff

### Over what specific time period were the 372,000 mail-in ballots in Pennsylvania rejected?
Answer: The 372,000 rejected mail-in ballot applications in Pennsylvania occurred from after the state's June 2, 2020, primary through at least mid-October 2020. As the article specifies, county offices like Allegheny County "rejected more than 49,000 duplicate ballot requests from the June primary to October 14."

Source URL: https://www.propublica.org/article/pennsylvanias-rejection-of-372-000-ballot-applications-bewilders-voters-and-strains-election-staff

### What were the primary reasons cited by Pennsylvania officials for rejecting mail-in ballots in the 2020 election cycle?
Answer: The primary reason cited by Pennsylvania officials for rejecting mail-in ballots was that most applications were duplicates, accounting for over 90% of the 372,000 rejections. This occurred because voters who requested ballots for the June primary had checked a box to automatically receive ballots for the general election, leading them to mistakenly submit additional applications. As the article states, "More than 90% of those applications, or about 336,000, were denied as duplicates, primarily because people who had requested mail ballots for the state’s June 2 primary did not realize they had checked a box to be sent ballots for the general election, too."

Source URL: https://www.inquirer.com/politics/election/pennsylvania-mail-ballot-rejections-duplicate-applications-20201016.html

### Did any official Pennsylvania government report or statement confirm the rejection of approximately 372,000 mail-in ballots?
Answer: Yes, an official Pennsylvania government report or statement confirmed the rejection of approximately 372,000 mail-in ballot applications. The Pennsylvania Department of State, which oversees elections, acknowledged the issue, stating that "some voters may have forgotten that they opted to be put on the annual mail ballot list when they applied for a ballot for the June primary," and that nongovernmental groups have created "confusion for voters and the likelihood that voters will not realize their application has been processed and they don’t need to submit another one."

Source URL: https://www.propublica.org/article/pennsylvanias-rejection-of-372-000-ballot-applications-bewilders-voters-and-strains-election-staff

